        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT2bc173c5f4",
          "@type": "regca:SupplyDescription",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT2bc173c5f4",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 196,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>The maximum supply of AUDT is 300,000,000. AUDT is minted as it is distributed. The maximum supply is allocated as follows:</p><p>(1) the Startup Offering: 3,500,000 AUDT (1.17%; 70 Units, each with 50,000 AUDT);</p><p>(2) the Tier 2 Node Offering: 33,500,000 AUDT (11.17%; 670 Units);</p><p>(3) the Tier 2 AUDT Offering: 22,083,333 AUDT (7.36%);</p><p>(4) AUDT Subsidies: 45,000,000 AUDT (15.00%), which is the most that the Subsidies Contract can mint and of which no more than 1,000,000 AUDT is paid before the filing of Form TR;</p><p>(5) MCV stakes: 500,000 AUDT (0.17%; 250,000 AUDT for each of the two initial MCVs);</p><p>(6) team: 30,000,000 AUDT (10.00%);</p><p>(7) advisors: 30,000,000 AUDT (10.00%);</p><p>(8) partnerships: 30,000,000 AUDT (10.00%), which is part of the Governance Council allocation;</p><p>(9) liquidity: 20,000,000 AUDT (6.67%), of which 12,000,000 AUDT (4.00%) will be committed permanently to three liquidity pools and 8,000,000 AUDT (2.67%) is for centralized exchanges;</p><p>(10) treasury: 56,500,000 AUDT (18.83%), of which 28,250,000 AUDT (9.42%) is the consideration payable to Auditchain for the Auditchain License and is deliverable after the filing of Form TR, and 28,250,000 AUDT (9.42%) is retained by us; and</p><p>(11) reserve: 28,916,667 AUDT (9.64%), which is not minted, is part of the Governance Council allocation and is held for the growth of the Network.</p><p>The Token Contract enforces the maximum supply of 300,000,000 AUDT in its code. No AUDT can be minted beyond it, and no upgrade of the Smart Contracts can change it. Each allocation can be minted only by the contract named for it, up to a cap written in the code, and no contract can mint from the cap of another or from what another leaves unminted. The caps add up to the maximum supply: the Key Holder Contract, until the Handover, 136,083,333 AUDT for our offerings, the MCV stakes and the liquidity and treasury allocations; the Subsidies Contract, 45,000,000 AUDT for AUDT Subsidies; the Claims Contract, after the filing of Form TR, 30,000,000 AUDT for each of the team and advisor allocations; and the Council Contract, after the Handover, 58,916,667 AUDT for the Governance Council allocation, which consists of the partnership allocation and the reserve. AUDT that a contract does not mint stays unminted. No other contract can mint it, and no proposal can move it.</p><p>After the Handover, the Council Contract can mint from the reserve on the two-thirds approval of the Governance Council, with no Governance Vote. It can mint from the partnership allocation only after a general proposal to mint a stated portion passes a Governance Vote and receives the two-thirds approval of the Governance Council (see \"Governance mechanisms\"). Each mint names the amount, the purpose and the contract that receives the AUDT. The Governance Council may deploy contracts for bounties, incentives and other purposes, and such a contract receives AUDT only through the Council Contract, within its cap. No AUDT from the Governance Council allocation may be minted to a member of the Governance Council, to its organization or to an affiliate of either, to us or to Auditchain.</p><p>AUDT is minted only on Ethereum. On Polygon PoS and Robinhood Chain, bridged AUDT is created only when the same amount of AUDT is locked on Ethereum, and is removed when it returns. The AUDT on the three networks together therefore never exceeds the AUDT minted on Ethereum.</p><p>No lockup or release schedule applies to the 8,000,000 AUDT of the liquidity allocation for centralized exchanges or to either part of the treasury allocation, with two exceptions: the 50,000 AUDT staked for our PVN is locked for two years like every other stake, and all AUDT is subject to the lock on transfers that is described under \"Lockups.\" The 12,000,000 AUDT for liquidity pools is held permanently in the liquidity contracts (see \"Plan of development\").</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 94
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT4b51215630",
          "@type": "regca:PricingDescription",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT4b51215630",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 281,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>We set the prices in our offerings. In the Node Offerings, we priced Units at $50,000 per Unit in the Startup Offering and price them at $62,500 per Unit in the Tier 2 Node Offering. Under Rule 101(e)(2) as proposed, those prices are the equivalent of $1.00 and $1.25 per AUDT. In the Tier 2 AUDT Offering, we intend to price AUDT alone at $1.50 per AUDT.</p><p>A purchaser of a Unit accepts obligations that a purchaser of AUDT alone does not accept. Those obligations are the two-year lock on the stake, the Conformance Validations and the fees of the MCVs. The equivalent prices per AUDT in the Node Offerings are lower than the price of AUDT alone.</p><p>We do not set the fees of the MCVs. We set and publish the preparation costs that Preparers pay us. Until the Handover, we also set the fee that a User pays for a validation and the other settings of the Smart Contracts, except the allocation of rewards, which we set only until the Governance Contract is deployed.</p><p>We accept payment in USDC or USDT, each taken at $1.00.</p><p>AUDT is not listed on any trading venue and has no market price. We intend to seek trading of AUDT after the filing of Form TR.</p><p>We intend to use 10 percent of the net proceeds of the Tier 2 Node Offering to buy AUDT in the secondary market, if and when one develops. No part of the proceeds of the Startup Offering or of the Tier 2 AUDT Offering is used to buy AUDT. The liquidity pools open at $1.50 per AUDT, the price of AUDT in the Tier 2 AUDT Offering (see \"Plan of development\").</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 95
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT2e0ad25804",
          "@type": "regca:LockupsDescription",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT2e0ad25804",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 282,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>Units. The 50,000 AUDT delivered with each PVN is staked and locked for a minimum of two years from delivery. If the Operator unstakes, the PVN is deactivated immediately and the Node License ends. A purchaser may sell a Unit after two years if a new Conformance Validation of the PVN is completed before the sale settles and the buyer is an Accredited Investor that holds an LEI or a vLEI. The two-year lock then restarts for the buyer. A Unit purchased without the Node License under the first exception may be resold only as described under \"Material terms of the covered investment contract.\" AUDT that an Operator stakes in addition to the stake of its PVN, to restore that stake or for the Preparer discount, is locked for two years on the same terms.</p><p>Tier 2 AUDT Offering. Of the AUDT to be sold in the Tier 2 AUDT Offering, 20 percent will be released at delivery and 80 percent will be released through the release contract in equal daily amounts over one year.</p><p>Transfers of AUDT. Transfers of AUDT are locked until the day named in the Form TR Signal. Until that day, AUDT can move only to and from the Smart Contracts: on delivery, on staking and unstaking, in payment for a validation, as a reward, as a penalty and on a daily release. It cannot be transferred from one wallet to another. AUDT can also move to the bridge, to create the bridged AUDT that the liquidity contracts on Polygon PoS and Robinhood Chain hold. A sale of a Unit after two years is not affected, because the stake moves within the validator staking contract. One movement between persons is possible while the lock applies: a User that engages a cohort of PVNs pays the Operators that it has chosen. When we give the Form TR Signal, we name the day on which we intend AUDT to be listed for trading, and the lock ends on that day. That day cannot be earlier than the day on which the Form TR Signal takes effect or more than 14 days after it. It cannot be changed once the Form TR Signal has taken effect, and the lock ends on it whether or not any venue has listed AUDT.</p><p>Team and advisor allocations. We may award rights to claim AUDT from the team and advisor allocations at any time, because the Token Contract, the Subsidies Contract and the Claims Contract are deployed. No AUDT can be claimed under such a right until after the filing of Form TR. We have not awarded any right to claim AUDT from the team or advisor allocations. The rights are recorded in the Claims Contract, which releases the AUDT of each award to its holder in equal daily amounts over two years and mints it as it is released. The two years begin on the day on which we give the Form TR Signal, which is the day on which we file Form TR, or, for an award made after that day, on the day of the award. The release continues if the holder ceases to serve us. We will award no right to ourselves, to Auditchain or to any entity that controls, is controlled by or is under common control with either of us. Individuals, including our officers and directors, may receive awards, which we disclose. We will award no right to a member of the Governance Council or to an affiliate of a member while the member sits, or within one year before or after.</p><p>Governance Council allocation. The partnership allocation and the reserve form the Governance Council allocation. No AUDT of it is minted before the Handover. AUDT minted from it after the Handover is subject to no lockup, other than the lock on transfers of all AUDT until the day named in the Form TR Signal.</p><p>Minting. The right of the Key Holder Contract to mint AUDT ends at the Handover. After the Handover, AUDT can be minted only by the Subsidies Contract, the Claims Contract and the Council Contract, each up to a cap written in its code, as described under \"Supply.\" No contract can mint from the cap of another, and no person can create a further right to mint.</p><p>Liquidity and treasury allocations. The 12,000,000 AUDT of the liquidity allocation that is minted into the three liquidity contracts is locked in them permanently, with the USDC paired with it. No lockup applies to the 8,000,000 AUDT of the liquidity allocation for centralized exchanges or to the treasury allocation, other than the lock on transfers described above and the two-year lock on the 50,000 AUDT staked for our PVN. That includes the 28,250,000 AUDT of the treasury allocation that is payable to Auditchain as consideration for the Auditchain License.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 96
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT0b806117a6",
          "@type": "regca:DistributionMethodsDescription",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT0b806117a6",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 283,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>Operators earn AUDT for validating machine-readable regulatory disclosures. AUDT is distributed in connection with that work in three ways.</p><p>(1) Direct payments. A User that requests validation pays the PVNs in AUDT, through the Smart Contracts, and/or in other digital assets that the PVNs accept as payment. There can be no assurance that AUDT will be accepted by PVNs. We are not a party to these payments, and we do not offer or sell anything in connection with them. We receive no part of them, except as described below for the PVN that we operate. Before the Tier 2 AUDT Offering, we sell AUDT only in Units. Until that offering opens, a User that does not operate a PVN cannot obtain AUDT, and a validation can be paid for in AUDT only by a person that has received AUDT as AUDT Subsidies or as a reward, such as an Operator or an MCV.</p><p>(2) Royalties. Royalties may be paid to developers of regulatory disclosure taxonomies. When a User requests validation of a disclosure that was prepared with a taxonomy developed by a third party, the User's payment is divided between the taxonomy developer and the other recipients described below. The third party may be a standard setter. A regulator that files its own model takes no royalty. At the date of this document, neither we nor Auditchain receives such a royalty.</p><p>(3) AUDT Subsidies. AUDT Subsidies are described under \"Plan of development.\"</p><p>Division of each validation fee. Each validation fee is divided among eight groups of recipients, which the table of network reward allocation names for a request put to the whole Network and for a request put to a cohort: the winning PVN, the corroborating PVNs, taxonomy developers, future subsidy replenishment, liquidity, governance staking, airdrops and grants, and the MCVs. The share of the corroborating PVNs is divided equally among the PVNs that corroborate the winning PVN on that request. The table states the percentages in force at the date of this document. The share of taxonomy developers is 10 percent in both cases. We set the division until the Governance Contract is deployed and can change it at any time until then. From that deployment, it is subject to governance, as described under \"Governance of the network reward allocation.\" The division in force is a setting of the Smart Contracts and can be read from the blockchain. It also provides how the share of a taxonomy developer that takes no reward is divided among the other recipients.</p><p>The shares for liquidity, governance staking, and airdrops and grants are held in the Smart Contracts. Nothing is paid out of them before the filing of Form TR. After that filing, their use is decided by a Governance Vote together with the approval of the Governance Council. The share for future subsidy replenishment is held by the slash contract and is paid out as AUDT Subsidies after the Form TR Signal has taken effect.</p><p>The MCVs receive the fees that Operators pay for Conformance Validations, their share of each validation fee and one half of the penalties taken from the stakes of PVNs.</p><p>The PVN that we operate competes for the shares of the winning PVN and of the corroborating PVNs on the same terms as every other PVN. We may therefore be among the recipients of those shares.</p><p>In the Node Offerings, we distribute AUDT only as part of Units, with 50,000 AUDT in each Unit. In the Tier 2 AUDT Offering, we intend to distribute AUDT alone, and one unit of the CIC will be one AUDT. The stakes of the two initial MCVs, which are 250,000 AUDT each, were allocated outside our offerings and without cost, for establishing the Network.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 97
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACTf4cf14023b",
          "@type": "regca:HoldingsByRelatedPersonsDescription",
          "dct:identifier": "FACTf4cf14023b",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 284,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>Related persons hold no AUDT. We have not awarded any right to claim AUDT from the team allocation (30,000,000 AUDT) or the advisor allocation (30,000,000 AUDT).</p><p>We may award such rights at any time, because the Token Contract, the Subsidies Contract and the Claims Contract are deployed, and no AUDT can be claimed under them until after the filing of Form TR. Upon the filing of Form TR, we intend to award rights to claim 7,500,000 AUDT to Jason Meyers and 1,500,000 AUDT to Thomas Egan, CPA, from the team allocation, and 600,000 AUDT to Avery Lindqvist from the advisor allocation. The Claims Contract releases the AUDT of each award in equal daily amounts over two years. The release of claimed AUDT is described under \"Lockups.\"</p><p>No related person has purchased a Unit.</p><p>We operate one PVN, staked with 50,000 AUDT from the treasury allocation. Like every other PVN, it receives a share of the AUDT Subsidies, which had reached 3,226 AUDT by August 31, 2027. We owe Auditchain 20 percent of those AUDT as royalty, which we will deliver in AUDT after the day named in the Form TR Signal. Before the Handover, we will mint to ourselves the remainder of the treasury allocation and the 8,000,000 AUDT of the liquidity allocation for centralized exchanges. We will mint the other 12,000,000 AUDT of the liquidity allocation into the three liquidity contracts, where it is locked permanently; we will not hold or control it. The AUDT that we will then hold totals 64,500,000 AUDT, including the stake of our PVN and not including its AUDT Subsidies. Of that total, 28,250,000 AUDT is deliverable after the filing of Form TR to Auditchain, a related person and the licensor of Pacioli, as consideration for the Auditchain License, and 8,000,000 AUDT is for our liquidity on centralized exchanges. Auditchain holds no AUDT at the date of this document.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 98
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT0eb82efe36",
          "@type": "regca:ReleaseSchedulesDescription",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT0eb82efe36",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 285,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>PVN stakes. When an Operator unstakes, the release contract releases the stake in equal daily amounts over two years.</p><p>MCV stakes. The stake of an MCV remains staked for as long as the MCV serves. When an MCV unstakes, the release contract releases the stake in equal daily amounts over two years.</p><p>Tier 2 AUDT Offering. Of the AUDT to be sold in the Tier 2 AUDT Offering, 20 percent will be released at delivery and 80 percent will be released in equal daily amounts over one year.</p><p>Team and advisor allocations. Claims are made through the Claims Contract after the filing of Form TR. The Claims Contract releases the AUDT of each award in equal daily amounts over two years and mints it as it is released. The release of claimed AUDT is described under \"Lockups.\"</p><p>AUDT Subsidies. AUDT Subsidies are paid through the Subsidies Contract, which mints them as they are paid, up to 45,000,000 AUDT. The Subsidies Contract caps the amount paid before the filing of Form TR at 1,000,000 AUDT. After the Form TR Signal has taken effect, AUDT Subsidies are paid first out of the AUDT that the slash contract holds, and the Subsidies Contract mints only the remainder. Our PVN receives AUDT Subsidies on the same terms as every other PVN.</p><p>Liquidity and treasury allocations. Before the Handover, we will mint 12,000,000 AUDT of the liquidity allocation into the three liquidity contracts, where it is locked permanently, and the other 8,000,000 AUDT and the remainder of the treasury allocation to ourselves. No release schedule applies to the AUDT minted to us, including the 28,250,000 AUDT of the treasury allocation that is payable to Auditchain for the Auditchain License.</p><p>Governance Council allocation. After the Handover, the Council Contract mints AUDT from the partnership allocation and the reserve as described under \"Governance mechanisms.\" No release schedule applies.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 99
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT3aa0c9bb34",
          "@type": "regca:MechanismsForGeneratingAndDestroyingSubjectCryptoAsset",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT3aa0c9bb34",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 286,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>AUDT is minted as it is distributed, up to the maximum supply of 300,000,000, which the Token Contract enforces. AUDT is minted only on Ethereum. The mint function of the Token Contract is called in four ways, each up to a cap that is written in the code. No caller can mint from the cap of another, and no upgrade can change a cap or the maximum supply.</p><p>(1) Validation rewards. The engagement contract triggers the Validation Contract. The Validation Contract calls the Subsidies Contract, which calls the mint function of the Token Contract. The Subsidies Contract can mint no more than 45,000,000 AUDT. It holds no AUDT: it mints each reward when the reward is paid, so that the AUDT outstanding on the blockchain can be compared at any time with the amounts that we report. After the filing of Form TR, the Governance Council may also direct the Subsidies Contract to pay consultation rewards, within the same cap and within the limits described under \"Governance mechanisms.\"</p><p>(2) Offerings and allocations. Until the Handover, the Key Holder Contract calls the mint function for the AUDT delivered in our offerings, the stakes of the two initial MCVs and the stake of our PVN, which is part of the treasury allocation. Before the Handover, it also mints the liquidity allocation, 12,000,000 AUDT of it into the three liquidity contracts and 8,000,000 AUDT to us, and the remainder of the treasury allocation to us. The Key Holder Contract can mint no more than 136,083,333 AUDT, which is the total of those amounts.</p><p>(3) Claims. After the filing of Form TR, the Claims Contract calls the mint function when AUDT is released under a right to claim from the team or advisor allocations. It can mint no more than 30,000,000 AUDT for each of those allocations.</p><p>(4) The Governance Council allocation. After the Handover, the Council Contract calls the mint function for the partnership allocation and the reserve, as described under \"Supply\" and \"Governance mechanisms.\" It can mint no more than 58,916,667 AUDT.</p><p>The right of the Key Holder Contract to mint ends at the Handover. In the Handover, before its administration key is destroyed, the Key Holder Contract gives the Council Contract its right to mint, capped at 58,916,667 AUDT. After the Handover, the Subsidies Contract, the Claims Contract and the Council Contract are the only ways to mint AUDT, and no person can create another.</p><p>The AUDT Subsidies paid before the filing of Form TR are capped at 1,000,000 AUDT. That amount is the capacity remaining under the $5,000,000 limit of the startup exemption after the Startup Offering and the allocation to the MCVs.</p><p>The four caps total 300,000,000 AUDT, the maximum supply.</p><p>On Polygon PoS and Robinhood Chain, the bridge of each network creates bridged AUDT only when the same amount is locked on Ethereum, and removes it when it returns to Ethereum. This does not change the AUDT outstanding.</p><p>No AUDT minted on Ethereum is destroyed. AUDT that is forfeited from a stake is reallocated by the slash contract: 50 percent to AUDT Subsidies and 50 percent to the MCVs.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 100
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACTffa1be8c1e",
          "@type": "regca:MethodsToVerifyTransactionHistory",
          "dct:identifier": "FACTffa1be8c1e",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 287,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "Transactions in AUDT are recorded on the Underlying Networks on which AUDT is deployed. At the date of this document, those networks are Ethereum and Polygon PoS. Transactions can be verified on the public block explorers of those networks (Etherscan and Polygonscan) at the block-explorer addresses given in the table of the subject crypto asset by network. The address of the Token Contract is the same on Ethereum and Polygon PoS and will be the same on Robinhood Chain."
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 101
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT2b2b315270",
          "@type": "regca:GovernanceMechanisms",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT2b2b315270",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 376,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>Governance Votes open when the Form TR Signal takes effect, which is after the Handover and after we file Form TR. From then, no code and no setting of the Smart Contracts can be changed unless two bodies approve the change: the holders of AUDT, by a Governance Vote, and the Governance Council. Neither can make such a change without the other. This item describes the Governance Vote, the Governance Council, what the Governance Council can do alone, and the rules for reporting schemes, on which nobody votes. Who holds the keys to the Smart Contracts before the Handover is described under \"Smart contract governance mechanisms.\"</p><p><strong>Voting power.</strong> Voting power comes from AUDT that has been delegated. A holder must first delegate its AUDT, either to itself or to another address, before that AUDT counts for any purpose of governance. Each delegated AUDT carries one vote. AUDT that has not been delegated carries no voting power. AUDT carries a vote when a person holds it in a wallet, or has staked it for a PVN, as an MCV or as a Preparer, and has delegated it. Newly minted AUDT counts like any other AUDT. AUDT that the release contract holds carries no vote until it is released. AUDT that is committed to a liquidity pool carries no vote, and AUDT that is held on a centralized exchange carries a vote only after it is withdrawn to a wallet and delegated.</p><p><strong>Proposals.</strong> Any address may submit a proposal if, in the block immediately before the submission, it held delegated voting power of more than 1 percent of the AUDT outstanding at that time. In this description, the AUDT outstanding is the total supply of AUDT that has been minted. It is not the maximum supply. An address may have only one proposal pending or open for voting at any one time. A proposal consists of a written description and a list of between one and ten actions. Each action identifies a contract to be called, the function to be called, the data to be supplied and any amount of native currency to be sent with the call.</p><p>There are two kinds of proposal. A \"settings proposal\" changes only one or more of the eight settings listed below, or the division of validation fees, and only within the range that the Smart Contracts fix for each of them. Every other proposal is a \"general proposal.\" A proposal is a general proposal if any of its actions is something other than a change of a setting within its range.</p><p><strong>Voting.</strong> Voting opens in the block after the proposal is submitted and remains open for 17,280 blocks, which is approximately 2.4 days on Ethereum. While voting is open, any address may vote once on the proposal, either for or against. There is no option to abstain, and a vote cannot be changed or withdrawn once it is cast. A vote may be cast directly or by a signed message that a third party submits on behalf of the voter. The weight of each vote is the voting power delegated to the voter as of the block in which the proposal was submitted. AUDT that is acquired or delegated after that block does not count for that proposal.</p><p><strong>Passing.</strong> After voting closes, a proposal passes only if (a) the votes in favor exceed the votes against and (b) the votes in favor are at least 4 percent of the AUDT outstanding. A general proposal passes only if, in addition, (c) the votes in favor are at least two-thirds of the votes cast. Conditions (a) and (c) are applied when voting closes. Condition (b) is applied once, when the proposal is placed in the queue, to the AUDT outstanding at that time. A proposal that fails a condition is defeated.</p><p><strong>Approval of the Governance Council, queuing and execution.</strong> A proposal that has met conditions (a) and (c) can be placed in the queue for execution only if it then meets condition (b) and the Governance Council has approved it, and only within 14 days after voting closes. Otherwise, it lapses. A settings proposal requires the ordinary approval of the Governance Council, and a general proposal requires its two-thirds approval. Both are defined below. The Governance Council may record its approval at any time after the proposal is submitted. It may withdraw its approval, by ordinary approval, until the proposal is queued. Queuing starts a waiting period of two days. After the waiting period, any person may execute the proposal. The timelock, which is part of the Governance Contract and has the same address, then carries out each action of the proposal in order. When a proposal is executed, the Council Contract checks the approvals again, against the number that was required when the proposal was queued. An approval no longer counts only if the member that gave it has since been removed or found to have come under an exclusion, or if it was given with an address that has since been replaced. A sitting member may add its approval until the proposal is executed. A proposal must be executed within 14 days after the waiting period ends. Otherwise, it expires and can no longer be executed.</p><p><strong>Cancellation and the Guardian.</strong> A proposal may be canceled at any time before it is executed in two cases. First, the Guardian may cancel it. The Guardian is the Council Contract, and it cancels a proposal when the Governance Council so decides by ordinary approval. Second, any person may cancel it if the delegated voting power of the proposer has fallen below 1 percent of the AUDT outstanding. A proposer must therefore keep at least 1 percent for up to approximately 32 days. The Guardian has no other power. No person is the Guardian, and we are never the Guardian.</p><p><strong>Scope.</strong> A settings proposal can change the following settings, each within the range that the Smart Contracts fix for it. We set the ranges before the Handover. This document does not state them.</p><p>(1) the maximum number of PVNs operating on the Network;</p><p>(2) the number of AUDT that must be staked to operate a PVN;</p><p>(3) the amount of the rewards paid to PVNs through AUDT Subsidies;</p><p>(4) the amount of AUDT paid by a User that requests the validation of disclosure instances;</p><p>(5) the allocation of the proceeds from data subscriptions between reporting entities and the PVNs in a cohort;</p><p>(6) the minimum balance that a Preparer must stake to receive the Preparer discount;</p><p>(7) the minimum number of PVNs in a cohort; and</p><p>(8) the minimum percentage of the PVNs in a cohort that is required to achieve consensus.</p><p>In this list, a \"reporting entity\" is the issuer whose disclosures are validated, and a \"data subscription\" is a paid request of a User for an analysis of disclosures. Until transfers of AUDT open, no proposal and no person can set the stake of a PVN above 50,000 AUDT or the maximum number of PVNs below 741.</p><p>A general proposal is required for everything else. That includes: any change to the code of a Smart Contract, including the Governance Contract and the Council Contract; a change of a range; a change of the voting rules described in this item, including the two-thirds requirement; a change of the seats, the thresholds or the powers of the Governance Council; a mint of a stated portion of the partnership allocation by the Council Contract; the admission or removal of an MCV; a budget for the Governance Council; a pause or a freeze beyond the limits described below; and a change of the rules for reporting schemes and consultation rewards. No proposal can raise a cap, create a right to mint, move unminted AUDT from one cap to another or mint beyond the maximum supply. The Token Contract enforces each cap and the maximum supply, and no upgrade can change them. A proposal to mint from the partnership allocation names the amount, the purpose and the contract that receives the AUDT.</p><p><strong>The Governance Council.</strong> The Governance Council has nine voting seats in three groups of three, and observer seats without a vote.</p><p>(1) Standard setters. Each MCV holds one seat for as long as it is an MCV. An MCV holds its seat itself and not through a delegate. If there are more than three MCVs, the MCVs choose three among themselves.</p><p>(2) Operators. The PVNs elect three Operators. The three seats are elected together, for two years. Each PVN has one vote and votes for one candidate, and the three candidates with the most votes are elected. A recall or a vacancy leads to a new election of all three. The rules for calling an election and for a recall are not yet written. A PVN that is admitted after the filing of Form TR votes only after one year. The first election is held before the Council Contract is deployed. Each vote in it is a message signed with the key of the PVN and is published, so that any person can recount, and the result is written into the Council Contract when it is deployed.</p><p>(3) Professional services firms. The members sitting may elect up to three firms, by two-thirds approval and only while at least four members sit. A firm must provide audit, assurance, accounting, legal or regulatory-disclosure services to reporting entities and must hold an LEI. A firm that operates a PVN, or that is an MCV or the delegate of an MCV, cannot take one of these seats. Each of the three seats runs in cycles of three years, whether or not it is filled. The cycles first end one, two and three years after the Governance Council is first seated, so that one seat ends each year. A firm that is elected takes the empty seat with the most time left and serves to the end of that cycle.</p><p>Observers. A public authority or a self-regulatory organization that oversees a reporting scheme carried on the Network may take an observer seat at its own request. An observer receives every proposal when it is submitted and may publish a statement on it. It has no vote and no key and receives nothing for the seat.</p><p>We expect the first Governance Council to have five members: the two initial MCVs and the three Operators that the PVNs elect. No professional services firm and no regulator has agreed to take a seat.</p><p>Who may not sit. None of the following may hold a seat, stand for election or vote in the election of the Operator members: we and Auditchain; any person that controls, is controlled by or is under common control with either of us; the founders, promoters, directors, officers, trustees and employees of either of us; the auditors, counsel, consultants, contractors and other advisers of either of us; the immediate family of any of those persons; and any person that has accepted an award of a right to claim AUDT from the team or advisor allocations. A person is not excluded only because it is an MCV, because it operates a PVN or because we consulted it on the four contracts of our sixth promised effort. One organization, together with its affiliates, holds no more than one seat. Seats are held by organizations, except that an Operator who is an individual holds an Operator seat in his or her own name. Each member that is an organization names the individual who signs for it, and that name is published. The exclusions of this paragraph apply to that individual. So do the bar on awards described under \"Lockups\" and the bar on consultation rewards described below. Mr. Meyers holds no seat.</p><p>How the Governance Council decides. Decisions are counted on the members sitting. A seat that has fallen empty still counts for 90 days, unless a successor has taken it. A member that has recorded nothing for 90 days is not counted until it records something again, and a member can record a vote against a proposal, or a sign of life, as well as an approval. \"Ordinary approval\" requires more than half of the members counted, from at least two of the three groups. \"Two-thirds approval\" requires at least two-thirds of the members counted, and it must include at least one standard setter and at least one Operator member for as long as a member of that group sits and may vote on the question. A member does not vote on its own admission or removal, or on whether it has come under an exclusion. With five members counted, ordinary approval requires three members and two-thirds approval requires four. The Governance Council can act only while at least four members from at least two groups sit.</p><p>How a seat ends. A seat ends at the end of its term; on resignation; when the PVNs elect anew; when an MCV ceases to be an MCV; when an Operator member has unstaked all of its PVNs; or when two-thirds of the members sitting, the member concerned being counted but not voting, remove a firm or find that a member has come under an exclusion. An Operator member stays until its successor is seated. A seat does not end because a PVN is deactivated. The holders of AUDT alone cannot remove a member. An Operator member or an MCV can replace a lost address with the key of its stake. The address of a firm is replaced by two-thirds approval.</p><p>Last resort. If fewer than four members have sat for 90 days, the members still sitting may, by more than half of them, name candidates for the empty firm seats, and the holders of AUDT may confirm them by a Governance Vote in which at least two-thirds of the votes cast are in favor. That Governance Vote requires no approval of the Governance Council and can do nothing else.</p><p><strong>What the Governance Council can do alone.</strong> Besides approving proposals and canceling them as the Guardian, the Governance Council has five powers that require no Governance Vote. The powers to pause and to freeze pass to it at the Handover.</p><p>(1) Pause. By ordinary approval, the Governance Council can pause any Smart Contract other than the Governance Contract and the Council Contract. A pause of the Token Contract, the Scheme Registry or the Consultation Stake Contract requires two-thirds approval. A pause of the Token Contract stops transfers and the minting of AUDT. It does not stop delegation or voting. A pause never stops the voting on a proposal, its approval, its queuing or its execution. All pauses together may last no more than 14 days in any 90 days. A further or longer pause requires a general proposal and lasts no more than 30 days each time. A pause that begins before Governance Votes open lasts until 14 days after they open. The Governance Council can end a pause early.</p><p>(2) Freeze. Upon a demand from law enforcement or an order of a court, the Governance Council can, by two-thirds approval, freeze the AUDT at one address or one stake in the validator staking contract. A freeze stops transfers and not votes. It cannot be placed on the address of a Smart Contract. It can be placed on the address of another contract, such as a liquidity pool, only by a general proposal. The Governance Council records the authority, the reference and the date of the demand or order, or a hash of them where publication is forbidden. A freeze ends after 90 days. The Governance Council can renew it once by two-thirds approval and can lift it by ordinary approval when the demand ends. A later renewal, or a new freeze of the same address or stake within one year, requires a general proposal. The Smart Contracts cannot verify that a demand or an order exists. Whether that limit is kept depends on the members.</p><p>(3) Its own affairs. The Governance Council elects and removes firm members, seats observers and finds that a member has come under an exclusion.</p><p>(4) Reporting schemes and consultation rewards. These powers are described below.</p><p>(5) The reserve. By two-thirds approval, the Governance Council can direct the Council Contract to mint AUDT from the reserve, up to 28,916,667 AUDT in total, for bounties, incentives and other purposes that it names, including to contracts that it deploys for those purposes. Each mint names the amount, the purpose and the contract that receives the AUDT, and is recorded on the Network. No AUDT from the Governance Council allocation may be minted to a member, to its organization or to an affiliate of either, to us or to Auditchain. No Governance Vote is taken on a mint from the reserve, and our votes cannot block it.</p><p>The Governance Council cannot change code or a setting, move AUDT or admit a PVN. It can mint AUDT only from the reserve and, after a Governance Vote, from the partnership allocation. Apart from those mints, its one power over AUDT is the consultation rewards described below. It cannot fix a defect in a Smart Contract. A fix is a general proposal, which takes at least approximately 4.4 days, and the contract may be paused in the meantime. The Governance Contract and the Council Contract cannot be paused. Every act of the Governance Council is recorded on the Network as structured data. Its seats are unpaid, and it has no budget in the code. The two initial MCVs received their stakes of 250,000 AUDT each from us, without cost, for establishing the Network, and we may give a later MCV its stake. MCVs and Operators earn the fees and rewards that this document describes. Its legal form has not been determined.</p><p><strong>Reporting schemes.</strong> No Governance Vote and no approval of the Governance Council decides what a reporting scheme says. The \"source\" of a reporting scheme is the regulator or standard setter that issues the requirement. Its \"developer\" is the person that files its machine-readable model; a taxonomy developer, as that term is used under \"Distribution methods,\" is a developer. Naming a source says where a requirement comes from. It does not mean that the source has approved the model or takes any part in the Network.</p><p>(1) Filing. A new reporting scheme, or a change to one, is filed in the Scheme Registry by its source or its developer, with a statement of what changes and of the rule or publication of the source that it carries. The Scheme Registry accepts a change to a reporting scheme only from the address that filed it, or from an address that its source has proven to be its own. Any other developer may file a rival model of the same requirement.</p><p>(2) Comment. Filing opens a consultation period of 60 days. During that period, any person may file public comments, including us and Auditchain. A comment may be a working proposal, such as added elements, sample filings or test results.</p><p>(3) Answer. After the 60 days, the filer files the final version with an answer to the comments, which states the comments that it took up, the comments that it did not take up, and why. The final version may differ from the filed one only in ways that those affected could expect from the filing.</p><p>(4) Effect. The Scheme Registry checks only the steps: that the 60 days have run, that the answer has been filed and what the scheme tests have reported. A version that has passed the scheme tests takes effect on the date that the filer names. The scheme tests are the published tests in force on the day of filing. The MCVs or their delegates run them against the Technical Specifications, within a deadline that we set before the Handover. No MCV and no delegate tests a reporting scheme that it or its affiliate has filed, or to which it or its affiliate has filed a rival. If no result is reported by the deadline, the version counts as passed. If a failure is reported, the report names the tests that failed and the version is held back for 14 days. It then takes effect unless the Governance Council has refused it. A PVN is not tested again for a new reporting scheme.</p><p>(5) Refusal and pause. By two-thirds approval, the Governance Council may refuse a filing, or pause a version for up to 14 days in any 90 days, only for a stated fault of one of three kinds. The first is that the version fails the scheme tests. The second is that it is not what it says it is, because it is not what its source published or because its filer is neither its source nor its developer. The third is that it did not go through the steps above. While a version is paused, the earlier version applies. The Governance Council cannot change what a reporting scheme says. The Smart Contracts cannot verify the stated fault. A member does not vote on a reporting scheme that it or its affiliate has filed, or to which it or its affiliate has filed a rival. That member is still counted, so that its exclusion never makes a refusal easier.</p><p>(6) Core and proposals. What the source requires is the core of a reporting scheme, and only the core can cause a disclosure to fail validation. An addition by a contributor is carried as a labeled proposal. A labeled proposal is optional and is never a reason for a disclosure to fail. It becomes part of the core only if the source adopts it.</p><p>(7) The process of the source. Where the source has adopted a requirement after its own public process, the consultation on the Network concerns only whether the model carries that requirement correctly. While the comment period of the source is open, a model may be filed as a draft.</p><p>(8) Urgent corrections. A filer may mark a change as an urgent correction of an error that makes valid disclosures fail or lets invalid disclosures pass. It takes effect once it has passed the scheme tests, and the 60 days of comment run afterwards.</p><p>The Reg CA Disclosure Model is entered as the first reporting scheme when the Scheme Registry is deployed. Its source is the rule of the Commission. That does not mean that the Commission has approved the model. Its developer is Auditchain, which takes no developer reward.</p><p><strong>Consultation rewards.</strong> After the Form TR Signal has taken effect, the Governance Council may, by two-thirds approval, direct the Subsidies Contract to pay AUDT to persons that comment on a reporting scheme (\"consultation rewards\"). The Governance Council is not obliged to do so, and we promise no reward. Consultation rewards are minted within the cap of 45,000,000 AUDT of the Subsidies Contract and reduce what remains for validation rewards. They cannot exceed 4,500,000 AUDT in total unless a general proposal changes that ceiling. Within that ceiling, we write into the code before the Handover a limit for all consultation rewards in total, a limit for one consultation, a limit for any 12 months and the minimum stake of a commenter. We have not yet set those four numbers.</p><p>For a consultation, the Governance Council may open a pool within the first 14 days of the 60 days. After the answer of the filer, it confirms the commenters to be paid and their shares. A commenter can be paid only on two conditions. First, it must have locked at least the minimum stake in the Consultation Stake Contract for that consultation, from the day of its comment until the answer is filed, and in any case for at least 30 days. Second, its comment must have been taken up in the final version, as the answer of the filer records or as the Governance Council finds by two-thirds approval. The stake is returned in full. A reward follows the contribution and not the size of the stake.</p><p>The following cannot be paid a consultation reward: the members of the Governance Council, their organizations and their affiliates; observers; the filer of the reporting scheme and its affiliates; and we, Auditchain and the persons that are excluded from the Governance Council because of their connection with either of us. No Governance Vote is taken on consultation rewards.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 102
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT8afb8826c6",
          "@type": "regca:SmartContractGovernanceMechanisms",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT8afb8826c6",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 377,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>Until the Handover, the Key Holder Contract holds our keys to the Smart Contracts on each Underlying Network. Those keys are: the right to mint AUDT for our offerings and allocations, which is limited to 136,083,333 AUDT; the administration key of the Token Contract, which can give an address a right to mint, within the maximum supply, or to freeze; the rights to pause a Smart Contract and to freeze the AUDT at an address; and, until the Governance Contract is deployed, the right to upgrade the code of the Smart Contracts.</p><p>From the deployment of the Smart Contracts on July 1, 2027 until August 31, 2027, we alone acted through the Key Holder Contract, and two of our three directors signed. Since August 31, 2027, when the Founding PVNs had been admitted, the Key Holder Contract has acted on the signatures of the Initial Key Holders. We are one of them, and Jason Meyers signs for us. The others are four Operators of Founding PVNs that are not affiliated with us: Ledgerline Compliance LLC, Fenwick Data Services AG, Orbis Disclosure Ltd and Kestrel Regtech Inc. Three of the five Initial Key Holders must sign, and we must be one of the three. That rule also applies to an upgrade of the code. If our key is lost, two of our three directors can replace it. No Initial Key Holder may sit on the Governance Council. An Initial Key Holder that is elected to it gives up its key before it is seated, and the Key Holder Contract, on the signatures of three of the Initial Key Holders, of which ours must be one, names another Operator of a Founding PVN in its place. The Key Holder Contract freezes AUDT only upon a demand from law enforcement or an order of a court. The limits on pauses and freezes that apply to the Governance Council after the Handover do not apply to the Key Holder Contract. A pause or a freeze that it has placed ends at the Handover.</p><p>Until the Governance Contract is deployed, we alone set the allocation of rewards, and until the Handover, we alone set the other settings listed under \"Governance mechanisms.\" Two of our three directors must sign, and we can change each of them at any time until then. Until transfers of AUDT open, neither we nor any other person can set the stake of a PVN above 50,000 AUDT or the maximum number of PVNs below 741. This document states four starting values: the stake of a PVN (50,000 AUDT), the stake of an MCV (250,000 AUDT), the penalties and the Preparer discount. The other values can be read from the Smart Contracts.</p><p>We will deploy the Governance Contract, the Scheme Registry and the Consultation Stake Contract on Ethereum, and the Council Contract on each Underlying Network, with the counsel of the Founding PVNs, after a second audit of their code. We will also deploy the three liquidity contracts, one on each Underlying Network, in which no person holds a key (see \"Plan of development\"). The first Governance Council is seated when the Council Contract is deployed. From the deployment of the Governance Contract, the right of the Initial Key Holders to upgrade the code ends, and the Council Contract is the Guardian.</p><p><strong>The Handover.</strong> Immediately before we file Form TR, we will carry out the Handover on each Underlying Network. In it, the Key Holder Contract first gives the Council Contract on Ethereum its right to mint the Governance Council allocation, capped at 58,916,667 AUDT. The right of the Key Holder Contract to mint AUDT, the administration key of the Token Contract and our right to change the other settings are then destroyed on each Underlying Network, and the rights to pause and to freeze pass to the Council Contract on each Underlying Network. The Handover cannot be undone. After it, we will keep two rights in the Smart Contracts. The first is the right to name the recipients of the team and advisor allocations, within the caps of the Claims Contract. The second is the Form TR Signal.</p><p><strong>The Form TR Signal.</strong> Several functions of the Smart Contracts wait for the filing of Form TR: Governance Votes, AUDT Subsidies above 1,000,000 AUDT, claims of team and advisor AUDT, consultation rewards and the end of the lock on transfers of AUDT. Only we know when we file. We will therefore give the Form TR Signal in the Smart Contracts immediately before we file Form TR. We will give it only for a Form TR in which we certify that we have completed our promised essential managerial efforts and that we are not making, and do not intend to make, any new promise of such efforts. The Form TR Signal names the time at which it takes effect, which is at least one day and no more than seven days after we give it. It also names the day on which transfers of AUDT open, as described under \"Lockups.\" We can withdraw the Form TR Signal until it takes effect, in case the filing fails. Once it has taken effect, it cannot be undone and we cannot use it again.</p><p>From the deployment of the Governance Contract until Governance Votes open, no person can change the code of the Smart Contracts or the allocation of rewards. From the Handover until Governance Votes open, no person can change any other setting. If we did not give the Form TR Signal, Governance Votes would never open.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 103
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT41f0db0c6a",
          "@type": "regca:PermissionsDescription",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT41f0db0c6a",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 378,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>Six kinds of privileged role exist or will exist: upgrade, admission, minting, the settings, pause and freeze, and the role of the Guardian. Each is described below, with its holder before and after the Handover.</p><p>(1) Upgrade. Until the Governance Contract is deployed, the Key Holder Contract can upgrade the Smart Contracts on the signatures of three of the five Initial Key Holders, of which ours must be one. We are one of the Initial Key Holders, and Jason Meyers signs for us. The other four are Operators of Founding PVNs that are not affiliated with us: Ledgerline Compliance LLC, Fenwick Data Services AG, Orbis Disclosure Ltd and Kestrel Regtech Inc. From the deployment of the Governance Contract, no person can upgrade the Smart Contracts until Governance Votes open. From then, an upgrade requires a general proposal that passes a Governance Vote and receives the two-thirds approval of the Governance Council. No upgrade can raise a cap, create a right to mint AUDT, move unminted AUDT from one cap to another or change the maximum supply.</p><p>(2) Admission. The MCVs admit a PVN to the Network after it passes a Conformance Validation. The MCVs also perform the annual Conformance Validations, deactivate a PVN whose deficiencies are not cured within 30 days and confirm that the buyer of a Unit that was purchased without the Node License qualifies. The two initial MCVs are named in the Smart Contracts at their deployment. No person holds a right to admit or remove an MCV before Governance Votes open. After that, an MCV is admitted or removed only by a general proposal.</p><p>(3) Minting. The mint function of the Token Contract is called by:</p><p>(a) the Subsidies Contract, up to 45,000,000 AUDT, for validation rewards, when the Validation Contract invokes it, and for consultation rewards, when the Governance Council directs it after the filing of Form TR;</p><p>(b) the Claims Contract, up to 30,000,000 AUDT for each of the team and advisor allocations, when AUDT is released under a right to claim after the filing of Form TR;</p><p>(c) the Key Holder Contract, up to 136,083,333 AUDT, until the Handover, for the AUDT delivered in our offerings, the stakes of the two initial MCVs and the liquidity and treasury allocations, which include the stake of our PVN; and</p><p>(d) the Council Contract, up to 58,916,667 AUDT, after the Handover, for the Governance Council allocation.</p><p>These four caps add up to the maximum supply of 300,000,000 AUDT, which the Token Contract enforces. Until the Handover, the Key Holder Contract also holds the administration key of the Token Contract, which can give another address a right to mint within the maximum supply. In the Handover, it uses that key once, to give the Council Contract its capped right to mint, and the key is then destroyed. After that, no person can create a right to mint. On Polygon PoS and Robinhood Chain, the bridge of each network creates bridged AUDT only against AUDT locked on Ethereum.</p><p>(4) Settings. Until the Governance Contract is deployed, we set the allocation of rewards. Until the Handover, we set the other settings. From the deployment of the Governance Contract until Governance Votes open, no person can change the allocation of rewards, and from the Handover until then, no person can change any other setting. Once Governance Votes open, the allocation of rewards and the other settings are changed by a settings proposal that passes a Governance Vote and receives the ordinary approval of the Governance Council.</p><p>(5) Pause and freeze. Until the Handover, the Key Holder Contract can pause a Smart Contract and can freeze the AUDT at an address. The limits that apply to the Governance Council do not apply to it. After the Handover, only the Governance Council can do so, within the limits described under \"Governance mechanisms.\"</p><p>(6) Guardian. The Council Contract is the Guardian from the deployment of the Governance Contract. It can cancel a proposal before it is executed, by ordinary approval of the Governance Council, and has no other power.</p><p>After the Handover, we keep two rights: the right to name the recipients of the team and advisor allocations, and the Form TR Signal. They are described under \"Smart contract governance mechanisms.\"</p><p>Two further roles appear in the table: the approval of proposals by the Governance Council and the filing of a reporting scheme by its developer. They are described under \"Governance mechanisms.\"</p><p>The table of permissions by role sets out each role and its holder.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 104
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT55358e063f",
          "@type": "regca:EcosystemTechnologyInfrastructure",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT55358e063f",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 457,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>The Pacioli Explorer is the entry point to the Network, and our aim is for it to become the global destination for disclosure and analysis. There can be no assurance that it will. It is built for three kinds of disclosure: the disclosures that issuers make under Reg CA, the monthly reserve reports that payment stablecoin issuers publish under the GENIUS Act, and the crypto-asset white papers that MiCAR requires. The Reg CA Disclosure Model is the first reporting scheme on the Network; the others will appear on the Pacioli Explorer once reporting schemes for them are added to the Network. Anyone can use it to look up an issuer and see each disclosure that the issuer has sent for validation, year by year. A color-coded indicator shows at a glance whether each disclosure is fully compliant, has only non-critical findings, is not compliant or is still being validated, and a corrected disclosure is linked to the one it corrects. For each disclosure, the Pacioli Explorer shows the PVNs that validated it and whether any of them disagreed with the result the others reached. It also links to the blockchain transactions that record the request, each PVN's result and the final consensus. Each PVN and each MCV has its own page. A PVN's page shows whether its Conformance Validation is current, the MCVs that attested it, the work it has done, the issuers and reporting schemes it serves, and its reputation score. An MCV's page shows the PVNs it has tested, how its results compare with those of the other MCVs, and its reliability score. Both scores are calculated only from records on the blockchain, using formulas published on the Pacioli Explorer. A map shows where issuers, PVNs and MCVs are located and how they are connected. A directory lists the providers of disclosure software and services and their status on the Network.</p><p>Pacioli is a web3 application based on Ethereum. AUDT is an ERC20-compatible utility token. The Smart Contracts are developed in Solidity and can be deployed to any blockchain based on Ethereum.</p><p>Pacioli has two main components. The first is LucaSuite™, the disclosure automation infrastructure in which disclosure models and machine-readable disclosures are created. The second is the PVN software, a logic and reasoning engine that reads and understands disclosures and detects inconsistencies in them.</p><p>The Smart Contracts allow a User that creates disclosures to engage the open Network of PVNs or a private cohort. The Pacioli Explorer gives the public access to validated disclosures.</p><p>The Pacioli Explorer is designed as a user-friendly, decentralized EDGAR. Each disclosure published on the Pacioli Explorer carries a canonical cryptographic proof, recorded on-chain, that links the issuer, the PVN that validated the disclosure and the disclosure itself. It also carries the proof that the PVN passed its most recent Conformance Validation and, where the statements in the disclosure describe activity on a blockchain network, the proof that ties those statements to that activity. Where the disclosure is filed with the Commission, it also carries the proof of its filing on EDGAR.</p><p>Through an application programming interface (API), wallets, trading platforms and other applications can show their users a validated disclosure, its compliance indicator and its proofs. They can also record that a purchaser received the disclosure, as filed, before completing a purchase (a \"proof of reliance\").</p><p>We license Pacioli from Auditchain under the Auditchain License, which is global and exclusive and includes the right to sublicense. The source code of the Smart Contracts and of the PVN software is public at https://github.com/Auditchain/. The Smart Contracts are open source, and the PVN software is public under the Business Source License 1.1, as described under \"Material aspects of the security of the subject crypto asset and the network or application.\"</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 105
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACT793b420aff",
          "@type": "regca:EcosystemParticipantTypes",
          "dct:identifier": "FACT793b420aff",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 458,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "<p>The participants in the ecosystem of AUDT are of the following types:</p><p>(1) MCVs, which are standard setters that specify data and taxonomy formats and act through the qualified delegates that they name (initially two standard setters);</p><p>(2) Operators, including us (we operate one PVN, staked from the treasury allocation);</p><p>(3) Preparers and other Users, which prepare disclosures and request validation;</p><p>(4) developers of regulatory disclosure taxonomies, which may receive royalties and may include standard setters;</p><p>(5) the sources of reporting schemes, which are the regulators and standard setters that issue the requirements, and the persons that comment on reporting schemes;</p><p>(6) the members of the Governance Council and its observers; and</p><p>(7) on the Underlying Networks, we and our affiliate Auditchain, whose validators and full nodes are described in the sections on issuer and affiliate blockchain network participation.</p>"
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 106
        },
        {
          "@id": "regca-1crypto:fact/FACTc08a4f2403",
          "@type": "regca:OtherPartiesAndSystemsUsingSubjectCryptoAsset",
          "dct:identifier": "FACTc08a4f2403",
          "sbrm-mm:sequence": 459,
          "sbrm-rev:isReportedBy": "regca-1crypto:party/5067001J8OL3RK15TE82/role",
          "cmns-dt:hasTime": "regca-1crypto:period/2027-09-01",
          "sbrm-rst:hasFactValue": {
            "@type": "sbrm-frst:NonNumericFactValue",
            "cmns-txt:hasTextValue": "AUDT is used only within Pacioli. It is not listed on any trading venue."
          },
          "xbrlb:sourceIndex": 107
        },
